[h1]Flavorium Universalis v0.4.0: Political Influence[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.4.* | Multiplayer synchronized[/i]

[hr][/hr]

Updated for EU5 1.4. The version jumps to 0.4 to match the game version it runs on. This release is not compatible with 1.3; the last 1.3 build is v0.3.12.

[h2]Political influence[/h2]

1.4 adds political influence as a currency for political decisions: granting privileges, replacing cabinet members, setting policies. The mod's own political actions now use it too, in place of government power, prestige or stability.

[list]
[*] [b]Appoint to the Household:[/b] 10 political influence. The gold cost is halved.
[*] [b]Take On a Protege:[/b] 5 political influence in place of government power. The gold cost is unchanged.
[*] [b]Dismiss the Rival Minister:[/b] 20 political influence in place of prestige, the same as replacing a cabinet member in vanilla. The estate penalty for dismissing an estate-aligned minister stays.
[*] [b]Replace the Appanage Ruler (France):[/b] 20 political influence and 10 government power. The noble unease modifier stays.
[*] [b]Confer a Charter of Privileges (subject ledger):[/b] 10 political influence in place of prestige.
[/list]

Event options that spend political standing to push something through now cost political influence as well:

[list]
[*] Granting the noble privilege and embracing the merchant compact (estate faction events).
[*] Granting minister funds with a comptroller.
[*] Supporting estate privilege reform and enacting the standing army reform. Both keep their noble satisfaction penalty.
[*] Siding with either rival minister, purging a rival, and publicly elevating a faction.
[*] Dismissing an insubordinate general.
[/list]

[h2]Advances[/h2]

1.4 no longer allows an advance to require an advance from an earlier age. Five subject advances were set up that way:

[list]
[*] [b]Federal Constitution and False Federal Charter:[/b] still follow their same-age prerequisite, so nothing changes in play.
[*] [b]Military March:[/b] still requires a Military Academy subject, which needs the earlier advance anyway.
[*] [b]Naval Administration and Puppet State:[/b] no longer require the earlier Naval Charter, Shadow Diplomacy or Client State advance. The AI still prefers Puppet State when it has Shadow Diplomacy or Client State.
[/list]

[h2]Earlier bureaus[/h2]

Most countries could not found a bureau until they embraced Confessionalism in the Age of Reformation. Two new administrative advances bring seven of the ten general bureaus forward. Embracing Confessionalism still unlocks all of them.

[list]
[*] [b]Chancery Offices (Age of Renaissance):[/b] unlocks the Board of Revenue, Privy Council and Audit Bureau, and adds 1 bureaucracy slot. This slot is in addition to the Age of Reformation one.
[*] [b]Offices of State (Age of Discovery):[/b] unlocks the Trade Commission, Colonial Office, Foreign Ministry and Admiralty Board.
[*] The War Council, Ordnance Board and Commissariat still require Confessionalism.
[/list]

[h2]Bureau Steward[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Reduced malus on the stewarded bureau:[/b] the bureau's negative modifier is 25% weaker while it has a steward. Like other cabinet action modifiers, this scales up with the minister's ability and cabinet efficiency. It never removes more than half the malus.
[*] The steward's +15% impact bonus no longer strengthens the bureau's negative modifier. It still strengthens the positive one.
[*] Shadow Court, Royal Trading Bureau and Scholarly Concordat now get the 25% upkeep discount when stewarded. Before, a steward there only gave the impact bonus.
[/list]

[h2]Game rules[/h2]

All of the mod's game rules have moved to the Flavour tab of the game rules screen, so they are in one place instead of mixed in with the General rules.

[h2]Factions[/h2]

The political parties from the Factions submod are now part of the mod, on a new Factions tab of the Court Ledger. The game rule "C&C: Political Parties" switches them on or off (on by default), independently of cabinet experience; with experience off, the Court Ledger shows only the Factions tab. Saves that used the submod carry on unchanged, and the submod's Workshop item is now empty and can be unsubscribed.

[list]
[*] [b]Concerns:[/b] each party watches three things of its own, such as court spending, army upkeep, estate taxes and privileges, its war record or how far society leans its way. The tab breaks every party's expectation down term by term.
[*] [b]What it grants:[/b] every effect a party can have is a card, in full colour when it applies and faded when it does not, and each party card lists the effects in play now.
[*] [b]Names:[/b] parties have plural names (Royalists, Magnates, Merchants, Hawks, Particularists and the rest) with variants by government, culture, religion and age: Statists in republics, Boyars, Grandees, the Ulema, Hats and Caps.
[*] [b]Map:[/b] the Court Ledger's map mode shades your cities by Liberal Thought.
[*] [b]Cabinet candidates:[/b] every party keeps at least two courtiers who could be appointed to the cabinet. Once a year a party that is short takes courtiers who joined it by chance from a party with three or more, and new courtiers are found for any gap left. If the party's estate cannot sit in the cabinet, they come from the crown or the nobles. Before, the smaller parties often had nobody to appoint.
[*] [b]Hawks:[/b] their standing grants +0.05 military tactics instead of +2% manpower.
[/list]

[h2]Schools[/h2]

Schools were a yearly experience bonus and little else. Each now has a seat, a curriculum, two scores, and projects to spend on.

[list]
[*] [b]Seat:[/b] a school sits at a university. If the realm loses that location or the university is gone, the school moves to another free university, or goes into exile until one is free. Exiled schools keep teaching their members but lose their bonuses and projects.
[*] [b]Kind:[/b] the seat's religion makes the school a studium, academy, madrasa, shuyuan, vihara, gurukula, yeshiva or school, each with its own bonus at the seat. Members who share the seat's culture gain half again as much experience.
[*] [b]Curriculum:[/b] five per discipline. Administrative: Law, Theology, Natural Philosophy, Medicine, Public Finance. Diplomatic: Letters, Rhetoric, Languages, Commerce, History and Genealogy. Military: Military Science, Navigation, Fortification, Gunnery, Logistics. Each gives a bonus at the seat and one across the realm, nudges society toward a matching value, and has its own disputation. Natural Philosophy needs quality 40, Medicine quality 50, Navigation a coastal seat, Gunnery the Age of Discovery. Changing it takes 10 years.
[*] [b]Quality:[/b] how good the teaching is. Set by funding, a Rector, a settled curriculum and senior members. It scales every seat and curriculum bonus (half strength at 0, one and a half at 100) and the members' yearly experience, and unlocks a fourth option in disputations at 60.
[*] [b]Funding:[/b] schools are paid for from the Culture budget slider. A higher slider raises the quality schools settle at and costs up to 5% of monthly trade and tax income per school. Before a realm can invest in culture, schools live on a free customary allowance.
[*] [b]Renown:[/b] how widely the school is known. It grows with age, the seat's development and literacy, and the realm's prestige and rank, and takes about a century to build, and is ranked yearly against every foreign university. Renown raises the realm's prestige and cultural influence and draws young scholars (who can become proteges), foreign students and artists. Falling far behind the leading foreign university costs masters and students.
[*] [b]Projects:[/b] Endow the Library (gold: research and literacy), Royal Chairs (political influence: experience for every member, more for proteges), Invite Foreign Scholars (prestige: institution progress at the seat) and Hold a Disputation (an event for the school's curriculum). One project every 3 years per school.
[*] [b]Rector:[/b] a new cabinet duty. The Rector's school gains double member experience, shorter cooldowns, and institution growth and literacy at its seat.
[*] [b]Schools tab:[/b] one large card per school with its seat (click to open, double-click to pan), founding year, founder and Rector, renown and quality with their sources, curriculum, funding and the project buttons. The leading foreign university is shown above.
[/list]

[h2]Patronage[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Works of art:[/b] the Patronage tab lists every work the realm holds, with its type, quality, artist and location. A new button opens the full Works of Art overview.
[*] [b]Unsellable works:[/b] an eye button hides what Sell a Work will not offer: priceless works, which are now marked, and monuments, palaces, temple towers, regalia and mansions.
[*] [b]Artists:[/b] each artist shows their patron and what they are working on, with its progress, or that they are idle. A piece with no progress is shown as stalled: works make no progress at all before the realm can invest in culture.
[/list]

[h2]Household[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Cards:[/b] the Household tab shows each post as a card in two columns. A filled post shows its holder, their seniority and years in office, and its effects at the strength this holder gives them. An open post shows its base effects and what it needs.
[*] [b]Appoint and Dismiss:[/b] open posts have an Appoint button that offers the four most senior ministers trained for the post. Filled posts have a Dismiss button. Appointing from a minister's own menu still works.
[*] [b]Tenure:[/b] each year in a post adds 2% to its bonus, up to 20% after 10 years, on top of the seniority scaling.
[*] [b]Retiring posts:[/b] a post that an advance you can already research would close is marked as retiring.
[*] [b]Within reach:[/b] a section, which can be hidden, lists the posts you could open by researching one more advance, and names the advance.
[*] [b]Office events:[/b] ten new events, two for each kind of post. Now and then a filled post raises one; a holder of seniority 5 or with 5 years in the post opens a better option.
[/list]

[h2]Balance[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Cabinet efficiency is four times stronger[/b] wherever the mod grants it: 12 minister effects and 55 country effects, including traits, offices, bureaus and parties. Ministers can now realistically reach two to three times their base effectiveness. For example, the Seneschal grants +32% at base, and the Intendant of the Household and the Permanent Secretary +40%.
[*] [b]Order and the Magnates:[/b] Order gives +10% crown power (was +25%), +5% estate maximum tax (was +15%), +50% cabinet efficiency and -15% nobility target satisfaction (was -5%). Magnates gives -10% crown power (was -25%), -5% estate maximum tax (was -15%), +10% nobility target satisfaction (was +5%) and +20% levy combat efficiency.
[*] [b]Junior Partner offers:[/b] the penalty to acceptance from the other country's tax base is lowered from 0.4 to 0.15 per point, so countries with a large tax base are less likely to refuse.
[*] [b]Rarer events:[/b] the young scholar a school attracts (now at most once a decade, and a third as likely per year of renown), The Knife (weight 8, was 20, with its own 20-year cooldown) and the rivalry between two accomplished ministers (20-year cooldown).
[/list]

[h2]Fixes[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Royalists, Absolutists and the Court Chamberlain had an effect that did nothing.[/b] They used crown agenda impact, which vanilla does not apply. Royalists now give crown power (+3% satisfied, -5% discontented), Absolutists legitimacy (+0.05, -0.1), and the Court Chamberlain +4% crown power.
[*] [b]The capital development trait event could offer only "dismiss".[/b] In the first two ages with a capital above 40 development, or the third and fourth above 50, no option applied. The event now offers the highest tier the age allows.
[*] [b]Removed the minister promotion popup.[/b] It reported a promotion that had already happened and changed nothing. Promotions show on the minister's card, and the branching ones still ask.
[*] [b]Missing diplomacy text:[/b] the Client State relation line and cancel option, and the Holy Protectorate request in the diplomacy screen.
[*] [b]A school's seat stayed claimed after the location changed hands.[/b] The old seat stayed gold on the map and its new owner could not seat a school there.
[*] [b]Up to three post-war military reform proposals fired at once.[/b] A victory now brings one proposal a year until one is taken or declined.
[*] [b]The Court panel's tabs could not scroll.[/b] The Schools, Patronage and Household tabs now do.
[*] [b]Choice events repeated the last option.[/b] When fewer than four people or works qualified (mentorship, envoys, patronage, sales, proteges), the last one was offered in every remaining slot.
[*] [b]Choice events could open with no option.[/b] If nobody qualified by the time the event fired, there was nothing to click. Each now has an option for that case.
[*] Several costs showed placeholder names such as "Cc office appointment". They now have names.
[*] [b]The Court panel's action buttons were missing.[/b] 1.4 removed the button type they used. All 19 are restored, with vanilla's tooltip showing the cost and why a button is unavailable.
[*] [b]The subject ledger could overdraw the treasury.[/b] Underwrite, Review and Festival checked for one gift's worth of gold but charged 2, 1.5 and 1.5 times that. The buttons now check for the full amount.
[*] [b]A script error at game start[/b] from the bureau counters, read by the Bureau Steward cabinet action.
[*] The minister seniority badge on cabinet cards is rebuilt on the 1.4 card layout.
[*] The Balance of Power progress bars no longer log layout errors.
[/list]

[h2]Localization[/h2]

French, German and Spanish text is updated for the changed costs and covers the new school, patronage and household content.
